#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Turn a Jetson Nano's JetPack 4.6.6 card into Ubuntu 26.04 on JetPack 5's
5.10 kernel: an image file to write over that card, or a second card
(docs/CONVERTER.md).

The new card boots Ubuntu 26.04 with its own systemd, SSH, the network
profiles and the accounts copied from this system; on its first boot with
systemd it builds and installs the GPU stack the reference card runs (JetPack 5's driver
set with CUDA 11.4, cuDNN 8.6 and TensorRT 8.5, CUDA 12.6 with cuDNN 9,
JetPack 4's TensorRT 8.2, both video converters) and the desktops, checks
them and restarts into the desktop's login screen.

Usage, on the Nano, booted from NVIDIA's JetPack 4.6.6 card:
    sudo ./nano26-convert --target-image ~/nano26.img [--kernel DIR] [--dry-run]
or with a second card in a USB card reader:
    sudo ./nano26-convert --list
    sudo ./nano26-convert --target /dev/sdX [--kernel DIR] [--dry-run] [--offline]

Checks, each a line, then NANO26_CHECKS=PASS or FAIL:
  root       running as root (the source card's table needs it);
  board      a Nano 4GB SD developer kit on the B01 carrier, the only board
             this project has tested (device tree compatible);
  l4t        L4T R32.7.6 (JetPack 4.6.6), with NVIDIA's bootloader package
             at 32.7.6, so the board's boot firmware is the one tested;
  boot       the running system's root is the SD card's APP (mmcblk0p1);
  source     the SD card's GPT: 14 partitions, APP first by number and last
             on the card, the 13 boot partitions before it;
  target     a whole USB disk, not the boot card, with nothing mounted or in
             use, 512-byte sectors and at least MIN_TARGET bytes; or a new
             image file, in a folder with IMAGE_WORK_MIN free to build it in
             besides IMAGE_RESERVE for this system (--size sets that working
             size; by default all the free space up to IMAGE_WORK_MAX);
  kernel     the 5.10 kernel folder (--kernel; build-jp5-kernel.sh's out/):
             every file matches its SHA256SUMS, Image is KERNEL_RELEASE, and
             the Nano B01 device tree and the modules with nvgpu are there.
             Without --kernel, once KERNEL_BUNDLE_SHA256 is pinned, the
             published download, fetched when writing, checked against that
             SHA-256, unpacked in /var/tmp and checked the same way; until
             then --kernel is needed to write;
  helpers    nano26-first-boot beside this script, and JetPack 4's USB 3
             firmware, which the new card's initrd carries;
  stage2     nano26-stage2 and its unit beside this script, and the
             repository files stage 2 runs (REPO_FILES): this script must
             run from a copy of the whole repository;
  nvidia-key gpg and gpgv, and JetPack 4's copy of NVIDIA's apt key with
             the fingerprint fetch-r35-cuda.py pins;
  accounts   at least one account to copy, so the new card has a login;
  clock      the date is 2026 or later (apt checks signature dates);
  network    the download hosts answer over HTTPS (skipped with --offline).
The plan then lists the new card's layout, what is downloaded and from
where, and what is copied from this system (network names are counted,
never printed).

Writing, after the user accepts NVIDIA's terms and types the target's name
(or passes --accept-nvidia-licences and --confirm NAME):
  1. the new card's GPT: the 13 boot partitions copied from the running card
     and read back, and APP from the same start to the card's end (for an
     image, to the end of the working size), formatted ext4 with the running
     APP's features, which U-Boot reads;
  2. Ubuntu Base 26.04.1, checked against its pinned SHA-256;
  3. Ubuntu 26.04's packages and Wi-Fi firmware, downloaded only, by 26.04's
     apt in a chroot, in one request: the boot packages and stage 2's (the
     desktops, GCC 11 and what the GPU stack needs from Ubuntu). apt and
     dpkg run on JetPack 4's 4.9 kernel (shown 2026-10-11), but systemd
     259 needs Linux 5.4 or newer, so nothing is installed here;
  4. NVIDIA's packages for stage 2 and Ubuntu 20.04's X server, in a copy of
     the repositories on the new card (/var/lib/nano26/mirror): NVIDIA's
     signed indexes checked by fetch-r35-cuda.py's and fetch-cuda12-probe.py's
     code against the keys they pin, then each package against its index
     entry and, where this repository pins one, its SHA-256;
  5. the kernel, its device tree, an initrd with the USB 3 firmware, the
     modules, and an extlinux.conf whose one entry boots nano26-first-boot
     as init; and stage 2 with the repository files it runs. On the new
     card's first boot nano26-first-boot installs the packages on 5.10,
     creates the accounts, switches extlinux.conf to the everyday entry (the
     same without init=) and restarts; on the next, nano26-stage2 runs as a
     systemd service (docs/CONVERTER.md, Stage 2);
  6. what is copied from this system: the SSH host keys, the hostname and
     the NetworkManager profiles (each skippable with --no-copy-...), and
     the accounts with their password hashes, groups and SSH authorized
     keys, the time zone and the locale, staged in /var/lib/nano26 for the
     first boot, which deletes the staged copies when it is done. Stage 2
     builds and checks as the account that ran sudo (else the first);
  7. for an image: APP's ext4 shrunk to its contents and IMAGE_ROOM, the file
     cut after it and the GPT written again with its backup at the new end,
     so the image fits any card of MIN_TARGET bytes; its SHA-256 beside it
     in FILE.sha256, both for the account that ran sudo only (the image holds
     the password hashes, SSH keys and network profiles). A failed run
     deletes the unfinished image. The first boot grows APP and its ext4 to
     the end of the card the image is written to.
The last line is NANO26_CONVERT=DONE or FAIL.

The target is never guessed: --target names it, it is checked again just
before writing, and only it is written. Serial numbers are not read. The
board's boot firmware and the running card are never written.

The record goes to /var/log/nano26-convert/<stamp> as root, else to
~/.cache/nano26-convert/<stamp>; it holds this output and every command's.

Testing on another computer: --root DIR reads /proc, /sys, /etc and
/var/lib/dpkg from a stand-in tree, and --source-disk FILE reads the source
card from an image file; with --root, a device target is only checked,
never written; --nvidia-test-plan FILE (only with --root) names stand-ins
for NVIDIA's servers, keys and pins. Written for the Python 3.6 of JetPack
4's Ubuntu 18.04.
